   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in relation\nto certain lung disabilities incurred by members of the New York city transit\nauthority in certain cases",
    "basePrintNo": "S6972",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6972-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §13-168.1, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6972\n\nSPONSOR: GOUNARDES\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to certain lung disabilities incurred by members of the New\nYork city transit authority in certain cases\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe bill creates a statutory presumption of lung disease that results in\ndisability or death being a result of the performance of duty for trans-\nit workers in New York city, as long as the disease was found to be not\npresent during the physical examination of the member upon entrance to\nthe occupation.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds a new section 13.168.1 to the Administra-\ntive Code of the City of New York to create a presumption of lung\ndisease related to the performance of duty of transit workers in NYC.\nThe section qualifies the presumption as existing only when an occupa-\ntional entrance physical exam did not detect any signs of the lung\ndisease.\n\nSection two sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York city transit workers perform physically exhausting duties in\nsettings where they are exposed to various environmental conditions that\na large majority of laborers do not have to operate in.\n\nIn these settings, transit workers are frequently exposed to a variety\nof biological conditions and pollutants that are found in subway\ntunnels, underground work sites and on mass polluted roadways.\n\nIn fact, in 2005, a scientific research study was funded by a grant from\nthe New York State Legislature through the New York State Department of\nHealth to the Transport Workers Union Local 100. The study, titled\n\"Health Impact of Urban Mass Transportation Work in New York City,\" was\nconducted by doctors and researchers from New York Committee for Occupa-\ntional Safety and Health, The City University of New York and Mount\nSinai School of Medicine. The study concluded that transit workers\ndevelop important, common diseases and injuries to which their work is\nlikely to be a causal or contributing factor. Such injuries and diseases\nhave been identified by a substantial body of scientific research,\nincluding epidemiologic and mechanism-based studies. The major health\noutcomes of concern are cardiovascular diseases, which include hyperten-\nsion, heart disease, and stroke; lung and bladder cancer and possibly\nother cancers; emphysema and asthma.\n\nThis bill will provide a necessary presumption for transit workers seek-\ning benefits or making claims of labor-induced medical complications of\nlung disease.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020: S6137A - Referred to Civil Service & Pensions\n\n2019: S6137A - Referred to Civil Service & Pensions\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nSee bill\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
